{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>And the anger burned, etc., against Moses.</b> I say that the fundamental consequence of His anger's burning is that Moses remained permanently afflicted with the defect of heaviness of mouth and tongue — and this is the meaning of saying <b>against Moses</b>, that is, in his very body; and this is correct. And the derashah of the Sages (Zevaḥim 102a) who said that it refers to His saying ״הֲלֹא אַהֲרֹן״ etc. — our words do not contradict that. And perhaps the verse alludes to both of them, hinting at them by saying <b>and the anger burned</b> (וַיִּחַר אַף) when it should have said ״וַיִּחַר ה׳״; and by saying ״וַיִּחַר אַף״ He alluded to two things." "<b>For he shall surely speak.</b> The reason for the repetition is to say to him: ״Speak now״ — ״he shall speak״ — meaning, even if he will be heavy of mouth and tongue like you, he shall speak. Or it may allude to our own words that we explained regarding the verse ״וַיִּחַר אַף״, namely His saying ״דַּבֵּר יְדַבֵּר״ — meaning, He said ״דַּבֵּר״ with reference to his own body, and He said ״יְדַבֵּר״ in order to append to it the word ״הוּא״, to say: he shall have clear speech and not you, for you shall remain as you are." "<b>And also behold, he, etc.</b> According to what was explained in His saying ״דַּבֵּר יְדַבֵּר״ — that it declares the praise of Aharon, that he would perform his mission even though he was heavy of mouth — it is fitting to say ״And also behold״ etc., for it is an additional praise." "And according to what I explained, that He eliminated from Moses the hope of removing the heaviness of mouth, it further intends along the lines of the statement of the Sages, their memory for a blessing, in tractate Shabbat 139a, and this is their language: ״In reward for ׳and he shall see you and rejoice in his heart׳ (וראך ושמח בלבו), he merited the breastplate of judgment upon his heart״ — thus far. According to this, He intended by saying <b>and also</b> (וגם) that besides eliminating from Moses the healing of speech as we have written, He also removed the priesthood from him and gave it to Aharon; and this is the meaning of ״And also, etc., and he shall rejoice in his heart״ — here He alluded to the removal of the priesthood, and these are the words of our Rabbis, their memory for a blessing." "<b>Further,</b> He intended to declare his praise and his humility, for it would have been fitting for him to take offense at being the mouth while his younger brother is exalted above him; and He said that besides not taking offense at the matter, but rather ״and also, etc., and he shall rejoice״. And by means of these words He declares that even though he is humble — ״he shall surely speak״ (דבר ידבר) — and even though he was heavy of mouth, as explained, humility does not cause distancing from doing the will of the King. And He said <b>and he shall rejoice in his heart</b>, to say: even though he will not show you joy outwardly, He, blessed be He, is witness that he will rejoice in his heart. Or it may mean not outwardly alone, but both outwardly and in the heart."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:24:38.127967007Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}